omputational model secondly  users can create hierarchical diagrams  where nodes do not only correspond to  neurons i will show an example to this  later  finally it is possible to add the  capability to change the model  parameters or computational models of  individual components  from the front end  now that i have presented the  capabilities we provide in the front end  i want to briefly talk about the  architecture of fly brain lab  so far i have talked about neura minerva  which forms the user side front end i am  presenting  at the top row the second row  i just unveiled shows what we have in  the user side back end  of these components the flybring lab  client  is the one that communicates with our  servers  we use the crossbar library to  communicate with the server  retrieve data parse it and send it to  minerva our server software is also  written in python and contains our  database  commands from the flybrainlab client are  interpreted  executed and the results are sent back  either at once  or in chunks when the data is too large  in practice we find the neuron  morphology requests and simulation  results retrieval to be  to such cases in which data needs to be  retrieved in chunks  importantly i want to mention that  flybrainlab is designed with modularity  in mind  supporting users and developers with  different technical backgrounds  with remote access to the vacant servers  anywhere in the world  on the left i am showing the different  proficiencies that are in demand for  helping with the development of our  platform  that those interested in helping  computational neuroscience can  contribute to  finally it is our hope that the  accessibility of jupiter lab as an  environment  makes it easier for newcomers to be  introduced to the modern challenges in  computational neuroscience way of  librarian lab  before moving on to examples showing  some sample circuits we have created and  simulation results  i want to briefly show the capabilities  we enable  via a number of utility libraries we  have created to better analyze the data  retrieved from the database during our  exploration  flybrainlab features a number of utility  libraries that  aim to make it easier to work with the  data retrieved from the servers firstly  there is the neural embed  library which creates node embeddings  for a graph in which neurons are nodes  and synapses are edges  on the left i am showing a bunch of  neurons closely connected with one  another  on the right i am showing the output of  one of the models implemented in neural  embed  which embeds neurons in a 2d space in  which closeness of nodes is highly  predictive  of their human annotated cell types we  have used heldard cell types to see how  model  how well our model worked in this  scenario these groups  can then be analyzed and interrogated to  see how  correct human annotations are or to find  new new types of cells  secondly we have the neurograph library  which allows for the discovery of  connectivity patterns  by using community detection or  clustering algorithms and specified  neural circuits  here i am showing the adjacency matrix  of more than twenty thousand neurons in  the hemibrain data set  ordered according to communities  extracted by applying the levain  algorithm using neurograph  we have more libraries for data  visualization  querying in python and for generating  morphometric measurements for individual  neurons  uh when that data is available such as  axon length  surface area or neuron value  with flybrainlab we seek to provide the  unified environment for the development  of brain circuit models  for the remaining part of this talk i  will go over two case studies of  circuits that we have  looked at as examples to the  capabilities of library and lab  the subject of our first study will be  the central complex  the sub-circuits of which have appeared  before in this presentation  we have created a highly complex circuit  diagram  that replicates our observations about  the circuitry of the central complex  we can highlight regions so by hovering  over them  or we can click on regions to ablate  entire regions at once corresponding to  multiple neurons  secondly our diagram as i'm about to  show in a second  is hierarchical allowing us to enter  into regions and observe how groups of  neurons are connected to one another  in these sub-circuits as you can see  in this quest we have started with a  work that was published in 2017  by former members of our lab  using a slightly different and  interesting input stimulus  now with an additional but stationary  bar  that is lower in intensity at 60 degree  angle to the fly  we can simulate the response of the  ellipsoid body  we find that the ellipsoid where the  activity pattern can still track the  location of the input pattern in the  scenario  we repeated the same process of model  development and simulation  for two other models of the central  complex from literature  shown here in the middle and right  columns  these three concurrently published  models each describe a different subset  of the neurons in the central complex  as visualized by the corresponding  neurons in the  in the bottom row in this video  we show three circuits side by side  showing the notebooks  the neural nlp morphology visualizations  and the circuit diagrams corresponding  to these three different models  note that for the specific circuit  diagram hovering over neurons  shows additional modeling information  for the loaded executable  model which is a feature of the circuit  diagram  towards the end of this video we also  show the simulation results and let me  fast forward to that section  and as you can see by running some  scripts we can generate these  visualizations  employing the methodology i have just  shown and using the same  input to all three models we perform the  comparative study of their input and  output behaviors we see that although  there are some slight differences in  their response patterns  all three models show the tracking  behavior of the ellipsoid body quite  well  we then looked at the the shared circuit  features that these models all capture  that allowed us to pinpoint the key  circuit features giving rise to this  behavior  we encourage you to read our preprint  for these details similarly  we can use flybrainlab to compare models  of the same species  across developmental stages or datasets  to shed light on the scaling principles  here for our second case study we have  looked at the earlier factory system of  the fruit fly  in three different data sets and used  connectivity patterns between the cell  types to construct  similar but slightly different  executable circuits  the earlier factor system of drosophila  consists of a number of parallel  subcircuits whose receptor neurons  express  different olfactory receptors olfactory  system of drosophila is intensely  studied  and we know a lot about the cells  forming the early olfactory systems for  both adult  and larval drosophila here i am showing  the neurons retrieved using  flybrainlab queries from two different  datasets we have  we have integrated known facts about the  circuit and our observations  from the query results to create  executable models  our model suggests that the activity of  the so-called projection neurons  downstream of the olfactory sensory  neurons in each of these subcircuits  are normalized in some sense and encode  the relative affinity of their olfactory  receptor and the delivered ordinance  to the affinities of all other olfactory  receptors  in both adult and larval flies  to be more clear we find that for  both of these circuits despite their  differences  the projection neuron or pn responses  are more similar to the affinity which  is in some sense  the input to olfactory sensory neurons  rather than the responses of the  olfactory sensory neurons themselves  whose activity patterns look different  this is  interesting as pians receive all factory  inputs from oss  directly all of these model development  analysis and comparison examples we have  went over  whether it's between models from  different publications using the same  data set  or between data sets or even between  developmental stages  are significantly accelerated by the  integration brought about by the fly  brain lab platform  recently we have used flybrainlab as a  tool for education on computational  neuroscience  in the computing with brain circuits of  model organisms course  offered in columbia university  thank you for listening to our talk i  want to conclude by stating that in this  age of widespread open data releases  there is a big opportunity in  computational neuroscience for exploring  mapping and understanding neural  circuits of the fruit fly brain  on a level of detail that will not be  possible for other organisms for years  if you are interested to learn more  about our work  you can read our preprints download fly  brain lab code on github explore our  website  or follow us on twitter and again thanks  for listening to our
 

